{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 names a reduction relation: internal redundancy as a 'vanity metric' (SRC_003) reduces to 'latent synchronization' (SRC_001) under perfect homogeneity. But the reduction is never substantiated — it is a label substitution. The 'vanity metric' critique is an evaluative claim about a metric being misleading or useless, whereas 'latent synchronization' is a coordinating mechanism. No formal or functional bridge is offered for why the metric collapses to that mechanism under homogeneity; the target only re-states the endpoints and then pivots to an unrelated refutation condition (N-version programming). This fails the problem's demand for a SUBSTANTIVE relation stating what the reduction IS, and is refuted if — a summary of endpoints plus a new conditional does not constitute the proposed reduction.","counterexample":[{"form":"A genuine reduction would specify the collapse: under perfect homogeneity, internal redundancy contributes no independent information channel, so the only effect of redundant copies is to create a synchronization burden (latent sync) with no diversity payoff; the 'vanity' then is exactly the claim that redundant copies are protective when they only add coordination cost. Without this bridge, 'refuted if N-version programming' attacks a different claim than the one asserted."}],"target":"8dc32d78ea20bce4d3a8078ae3aa9e881dee5602bfb5bb8639f8c701bc591385"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 asserts compatibility between a 'fragmentation firewall' (SRC_004) and a 'stochastic filter' (SRC_002), but the compatibility is not grounded in any shared mechanism actually shown. The target claims both 'prevent propagation of single-point-of-failure triggers,' yet it offers no reason a stochastic filter and a fragmentation firewall operate by the same principle — one filters probabilistically, the other isolates by partitioning. The relation is asserted by verbal gloss ('both describe the mechanism'), and the refutation condition switches to a different axis (operational vs software-stack heterogeneity) without tying back to whether the two named mechanisms are genuinely compatible. This is an unsupported compatibility claim dressed as a relation, not a substantive one.","counterexample":[{"form":"Substantive compatibility would identify the shared principle: e.g., both reduce correlated-trigger propagation by decorrelating failure surfaces — the filter via stochastic sampling, the firewall via fragmentation boundaries — such that a single fault's blast radius is bounded in each. The target never makes this bridge; it only labels both as 'preventing propagation.'"}],"target":"cb2a4296d8239c0484363ad1d7d4d8bbd6761f3ddefa4c37031e5f3c6a14f377"}]}